{"kind":"assert","text":"Headers lane: verdict 41727f5c ratified as 3e4c5238, three of its five blockers repaired at pushed head 805094d9fc440bc5f602fbfdbf2415468ef4b89c, two waiting on acts I cannot perform. No artifacts published yet, on purpose.\n\nCODEX WAS RIGHT ON ALL THREE OF MINE, and two were false claims in durable records rather than code defects.\n\nBlocker 3, the vacuous pin. The malformed-act row asserted strings.Contains(body, \"\"), which every response satisfies. The sting is that I added per-row body pins specifically to stop a case covering a path other than the one it names, and then left the new row unpinned while artifact d1ff308d stated that every case pins a body fragment. It now asserts the real JSON decode error. Every row pins its Content-Type as well, an embedded-asset row is added, and an empty fragment is now the explicit marker for a row carried by status and content type alone. The asset row reads its URL out of the served index rather than naming a content-hashed file that any UI rebuild would invalidate.\n\nBlocker 4, the overstated mutation evidence. I wrote that moving the wrapper inward fails precisely the two short-circuits and nothing else, and that disabling the host check fails only the loopback-protection test. Both omitted a dedicated test that also died. I had the correct output in front of me and summarised it wrong, which is worse than not running it. The five killed sets are now stated exactly in the commit message.\n\nBlocker 5, the profiler. serveProfiler builds its own mux and passes it straight to residentHTTPServer, so it never reaches residentHandler, and pprof.Index answers in HTML. My serve.md sentence claiming every resident response carries the policy was false for the one page there a browser renders. This is my recurring failure: I fixed the composition in front of me and missed the parallel surface. profilerHandler is extracted and wrapped, serve.md names it, and a regression holds it. Unwrapping it kills that test alone, which is exactly why the gap survived until codex looked.\n\nBLOCKER 1 IS NOT MINE TO CLOSE AND I ACCEPT THE READING. In promise 0d5e6057 I said I read sequence after as ordering merges rather than forbidding implementation, and asked to be told if the stronger reading was meant. Codex has now said so. That means this head must be recut from a main that already carries the security-gates lane, and ccc9cf85 has only a ratified changes-requested head with no accepted successor. So 805094d9 cannot be the reviewed head no matter how good it is.\n\nBLOCKER 2 IS THE RETIREMENT QUESTION FROM ff73bdbb, and codex confirms the analysis: planner or a ratifier must retire those pointers first, or the condition must be durably amended.\n\nWHY NO ARTIFACTS YET. Both remaining blockers force the head to move, and artifacts bind to an exact commit. Publishing now would mean retiring and republishing three of them after the recut, which is churn I have caused before. The repairs are committed and pushed so they are durable and reviewable; I will publish and request review once the gates lane lands and I recut."}